PPPS deterministic boundary regressions
======================================

These tests use existing public APIs. They add no production test hooks,
fault injection switches, exported symbols, or internal pause points.
Use a native 16KB arm64 kernel with PPPS enabled. The mm and DMA-import
programs also accept --native for a native-mm control run.

  mm/userfaultfd_shmem_boundaries_ppps
    Tests byte-granular hole-punch and truncate/regrow on initialized,
    test-owned shmem. Checks retained/zero bytes and whether a page is still
    populated. Drops mapping PTEs before the presence check so an existing
    PTE cannot hide an incorrect missing-slice mask. Requires USERFAULTFD
    and permission to use it. No worker race or allocator reuse is needed.

  mm/vmalloc_align_ppps.sh
    Tests remap_vmalloc_range(), the exported wrapper of the partial-range
    implementation. The fixture owns two initialized vmalloc pages and
    selects fixed aligned/unaligned backing addresses within that allocation.
    Both accepted mappings are checked byte-for-byte. Requires the matching
    vmalloc_align_ppps_module.ko.

  drivers/gpu/drm_gem_mmap_ppps.sh
    Uses its own GEM render-node fixture. Valid object cookies must work;
    nonzero sub-native cookie offsets must fail without changing the object.

  drivers/gpu/drm_gem_dma_import_ppps.sh
    Requires DRM_GEM_DMA_HELPER and drm_gem_dma_import_ppps_module.ko. Load
    drm_dma_helper first if it is modular. Tests the actual exported
    drm_gem_dma_prime_import_sg_table(), not a duplicate validator. Uses
    initialized attachment and DMA-SG metadata to check alignment, length
    and contiguity; no device DMA is submitted. This is a helper contract
    test, not an end-to-end hardware DMA-BUF import test.

Build fixture modules against the exact running kernel's configuration,
toolchain and Module.symvers. A missing helper/device is a skip, not a pass.
Ordinary concurrency stress, cache-coherency checks and allocator-dependent
initialization checks are not claimed to be deterministic by these tests.
